adjudication.rationale:
In the new `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts` handler, decryption/parsing is done as:

```ts
const keys = JSON.parse(
  symmetricDecrypt(reqBody.keys, process.env.CALCOM_APP_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY || "")
);
```

This (1) falls back to `""` when `CALCOM_APP_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY` is unset, so the endpoint will still attempt to decrypt attacker-controlled `reqBody.keys` with an empty key rather than failing closed, and (2) neither `symmetricDecrypt(...)` nor `JSON.parse(...)` is wrapped in a try/catch, so malformed input (or a missing/invalid key) can throw and produce an unhandled 500 error path. Both behaviors are directly verifiable in the diff and represent a correctness/security robustness issue.

Yes. In `packages/app-store/_utils/oauth/parseRefreshTokenResponse.ts`, after validating the token response it explicitly does:

```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```

That inserts a literal placeholder string when the upstream refresh response omits `refresh_token` (a common OAuth behavior). This is not preserving the prior refresh token; it fabricates a bogus value. Since callers (e.g., Office365CalendarService) merge parsed data into stored credentials and then persist them, this can lead to storing an unusable refresh token and breaking future refreshes—i.e., silent credential corruption.

In `packages/app-store/_utils/oauth/parseRefreshTokenResponse.ts`, after parsing the token response it explicitly does:

```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```

So when the refresh token is absent it fabricates a literal placeholder string rather than failing or preserving the previous real refresh token. That is a concrete correctness risk: any later logic that attempts to refresh using the stored `refresh_token` will use an invalid value, leading to delayed authentication/refresh failures instead of an immediate, explicit error.
